  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given two positive integers NNN and XXX.\n\nFind any permutation PPP of the integers 111 to NNN such that the number of indices iii (1≤i≤N1 \\leq i \\leq N1≤i≤N) satisfying\n\nPi∣i=XP_i \\mid i = XPi​∣i=Xis as large as possible.\n\nHere, ∣\\mid∣ denotes the bitwise OR operation.\n\nA permutation of the integers 111 to NNN is an array of length NNN that contains every integer from 111 to NNN exactly once.\n\nIf there are multiple permutations that maximize the required count of indices, you may find any of them.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and XXX – the length of the permutation, and the target bitwise OR.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line NNN space-separated integers, denoting the permutation you found.\n\nIf there are multiple valid answers, all of them will be accepted.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤2⋅1051 \\leq N \\leq 2\\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "1≤X&lt;2N1 \\leq X \\lt 2N1≤X&lt;2N",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n5 1\n3 4\n4 6\n6 5",
        "output": "1 3 2 5 4\n3 1 2\n3 4 1 2\n5 6 3 1 4 2",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: We have N=5N = 5N=5 and X=1X = 1X=1. With P=[1,3,2,5,4]P = [1, 3, 2, 5, 4]P=[1,3,2,5,4], we have P1∣1=1∣1=1P_1 \\mid 1 = 1 \\mid 1 = 1P1​∣1=1∣1=1, so there's one valid index.\nIt can be shown that it's not possible to obtain more than one valid index.\nTest case 222: No matter what permutation is taken, there will be no indices satisfying Pi∣i=4P_i\\mid i = 4Pi​∣i=4, so any permutation of [1,2,3][1, 2, 3][1,2,3] is a valid answer here.\nTest case 333: With P=[3,4,1,2]P = [3, 4, 1, 2]P=[3,4,1,2], there are two indices (i=2i = 2i=2 and i=4i = 4i=4) for which Pi∣i=XP_i \\mid i = XPi​∣i=X. This is optimal."
      }
